  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Rolls Royce obligated $830M in federal contracts in FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 38.4% year over year. Rolls Royce won 313 awards so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $2.7M. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $794M. Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing is the largest market for Rolls Royce, accounting for $746M in obligations. Other notable buyers include the Department of Homeland Security and NASA, which obligated $34.7M and $430K respectively. Recent fiscal-year history shows variability, with obligations ranging from $860M in FY24 to $1.3B in FY25. The mix of markets shows that most obligations flow to engine-related manufacturing and air-transport support activities, while engineering services and research account for smaller shares. These totals are fiscal year to date and represent contract obligations rather than total contract values, except where contract tables list award-value figures separately. The count and average award size reflect the current-year portfolio through FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest federal buyers of Rolls Royce?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $794M. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $34.7M, and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $430K. These three agencies account for the bulk of FY2026 to date obligations for Rolls Royce."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What parts of the business drive federal obligations?",
        "body": "Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing drove $746M in obligations. Other Support Activities for Air Transportation and Aircraft Manufacturing contributed $34.6M and $26.4M respectively, with smaller amounts in fabricated metal products, engineering services, and other technical categories."
      }
    ]
  },
